          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do European and US bodies differ on when to start medication for stage 1 hypertension?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Yes, the sources I found describe a disagreement. But they don't give enough detail to compare the two positions on when to start medication, and they don't report how strong the evidence is.\n\n**Where they differ (per the retrieved summaries):**\n- **Threshold.** US guidance classes 130–139 / 80–89 mm Hg as stage 1 hypertension. European guidance starts office hypertension at 140/90 mm Hg and calls the 130–139 / 80–89 band \"high-normal,\" not stage 1 disease. The same reading is labeled differently depending on which body's guidance you use. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.)\n- **Low-risk treatment.** The summaries say bodies differ on how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension. Much of the population US guidance calls stage 1 is advised on lifestyle alone under European guidance. (Source: *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*.)\n\n**Where US guidance stands (per the retrieved summary):**\n- Stage 1 with established cardiovascular disease, diabetes, chronic kidney disease, or an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10% or more: drug treatment alongside lifestyle changes.\n- Stage 1 without those features: lifestyle measures first, with reassessment after three to six months before drug treatment is considered.\n\n**What I could not find:**\n- **A European drug-start rule for stage 1 or grade 1 hypertension.** The retrieved European material only states the threshold difference and that the approach differs. It does not give a risk-based rule for starting medication.\n- **The strength of trial evidence.** My search for this returned no relevant passages, so I can't say how strong the evidence is for either position. The summaries also don't name the specific societies behind them.\n\nFor a clear comparison of the two positions and their evidence, you would need the full European and US guideline documents, which I don't have here. Your own situation, including your risk factors, would decide which recommendation applies to you, so it is worth raising with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
